   $q$In which sentence does the transition express a relationship that does not fit the ideas it connects?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The choir rehearsed for months; consequently, its spring concert ran smoothly.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-cause-effect"},{"text":"The bridge was closed for repairs; similarly, drivers took a longer route along the river.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The trail is steep near the summit; nonetheless, many beginners complete it each summer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-concession"},{"text":"The library added evening hours; as a result, more working adults began borrowing books.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-cause-effect"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs; similarly, drivers took a longer route along the river.$q$, $q$The bridge closure caused drivers to take a longer route, so a cause word such as "consequently" is needed; "similarly" wrongly suggests the two facts are parallel. The trail sentence correctly uses "nonetheless" because beginners succeed despite the steep climb.$q$, 'draft', null)
