-- Passage wg12-writing-with-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('90aee6ac-600b-53b0-917e-2e2927ad6d83', 'wg12-writing-with-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: A Bridge That Reports on Itself$q$, $q$(1) In the article "A Bridge That Reports on Itself," structural engineer Farida Haddad describes how forty small sensors were attached to the Kessel River Bridge in 2020. (2) The sensors measure how much the steel stretches and vibrates as traffic crosses, and they send readings to an engineering office every ten minutes. (3) Before the sensors, inspectors climbed the bridge once every two years and judged its condition mostly by eye. (4) Haddad argues that the sensors do not replace inspectors but instead tell them where to look. (5) In 2022, the readings showed that one joint was vibrating more on cold mornings than on warm afternoons. (6) An inspection team was sent to that joint and found a cracked bearing that had not appeared in the previous visual report.

(7) This example shows that sensors are good. (8) ____, Haddad cautions that the system produced more than two hundred false alarms in its first year, mostly set off by heavy trucks and strong wind. (9) Engineers had to study months of readings before they could tell a real warning from ordinary noise. (10) The bridge is about 1,200 feet long and first opened to traffic in 1958. (11) These false alarms help explain why Haddad insists that people, not software, must make the final decisions about repairs.

(12) In conclusion, technology is changing the world in many ways.$q$, 'draft')
