insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('75148bc6-f525-5088-86a9-1ccd2e3c3fb6', 'wg12-writing-with-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Counting What Cannot Be Counted$q$, $q$(1) Noor Halabi's poem "Inventory" is spoken by a woman counting the stock in her father's hardware store on the night before it closes for good. (2) The poem is written as a list, and Halabi uses that plain form to show that the speaker cannot reduce the store to objects that can be counted. (3) The first stanza sounds like an ordinary record: "forty hinges, brass; nine coils of rope; a drawer of keys with no locks." (4) Even here, though, the "keys with no locks" hint at things that have lost the purpose they once served. (5) ____, the items begin to slip from objects into memories.

(6) The second stanza lists "one stool, its seat worn pale by forty years of waiting customers," and "the bell above the door, which knew every name." (7) These lines still follow the pattern of a count, but the details describe people and time instead of goods. (8) Hardware stores often sell paint, lumber and garden supplies as well as tools. (9) By the final stanza, the speaker gives up counting altogether. (10) The poem ends, "I write: one store. I cannot make the number smaller." (11) ____

(12) In conclusion, "Inventory" is a sad poem about a store closing.$q$, 'draft')
