  ('94a60f9b-a4f0-5acc-8aba-c4edf69a0721', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'data-statistics'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Find the quartiles by leaving the median out of both halves. What is the interquartile range of the data set 2, 4, 5, 7, 9, 12, 14, 18, 21?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$7$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subtracted-median-from-q3"},{"text":"$19$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"computed-range-instead"},{"text":"$9$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"included-median-in-halves"},{"text":"$11.5$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$11.5$$q$, $q$There are 9 values, so the median is the fifth value, $9$. Leave it out of both halves. The lower half 2, 4, 5, 7 has $Q_1 = \frac{4 + 5}{2} = 4.5$, and the upper half 12, 14, 18, 21 has $Q_3 = \frac{14 + 18}{2} = 16$. The IQR is $16 - 4.5 = 11.5$. The value $9$ comes from putting the median into both halves, which gives $Q_1 = 5$ and $Q_3 = 14$.$q$, 'draft', null)
